{"instruction": "ImgBurn verify fails at LBA 614 (\\INIT\\YAMADA.BIN, offset 2072) after applying a CSR+ layer, but the disc boots fine on a real PS2. What causes this and how do I fix it properly?", "input": "notes/2026-07-27-imgburn-verify-yamada.md", "output": "<thinking>\nOffset 2072 within a 2352-byte MODE2/2352 sector is the EDC field\n(USER_OFF=24 + USER=2048 = 2072), confirmed against Neill Corlett's public-domain\nMode2 Form1 layout used in scripts/repair_mode2_edc.py. Pristine has EDC\n`cca1b464` at LBA 614; the builder output has `00000000` — a zeroed footer, not\ncorrupted user data (the note confirms user data is byte-identical to pristine).\nPS2 boots anyway because MechaPwn/the drive either tolerates a stale EDC or the\nburner regenerates checksums on write; ImgBurn's verify pass, however, compares\nbyte-for-byte against the *image*, so a zeroed footer in the image always fails\nverify regardless of what lands on the disc. The durable fix is not to reburn and\nhope — it's to regenerate real EDC/ECC for every sector whose user data changed\nbefore shipping, which is exactly what `repair_mode2_edc.py` (Mode2 Form1 EDC via\nCRC32-like polynomial + P/Q Reed-Solomon ECC) does, matching the builder's own\n`builder/edc.js` fix mentioned in the note.\n</thinking>\n```bash\npython3 scripts/repair_mode2_edc.py \\\n    --pristine pristine/FINALFANTASY7_D1.bin \\\n    --input cache/csr/FINALFANTASY7_D1.bin \\\n    --output cache/csr/FINALFANTASY7_D1.bin --in-place\n```\nThis restores unchanged sector footers from pristine and recomputes EDC/ECC only\nfor sectors whose 2048-byte user data actually differs, so ImgBurn verify passes\nwithout altering any gameplay-relevant bytes."}
{"instruction": "Where in FIELD.BIN is the per-field LBA/size index located, and what's the record format?", "input": "Qhimm forum: FIELD.BIN (exploration) thread, topic=6496.0", "output": "<thinking>\nPer the Qhimm forums (forums.qhimm.com/index.php?topic=6496.0, CybEdit), FIELD.BIN\nsits at ISO sector 55000 as the first record in the \\FIELD directory. Its internal\nindex table runs from file offset 0x0003A5B8 to 0x0003EF80, holding 2361 pairs of\nlittle-endian uint32: (Logical Sector Number, Size), one pair per FIELD file (.DAT/\n.MIM/.BSX) — this table does NOT include the BCX (battle character) files, which\nget their own separate index at 0x0003F08C-0x0003F0DC. The engine walks this table\nto resolve which sector to seek to when loading a field map's assets, ordered\nDAT/MIM/BSX per location. This matches the little-endian-pointer convention used\nthroughout the MIPS R3000A/PSX binary formats this repo's ISO tooling\n(`scripts/psx_mode2_iso.py`) already assumes (`_u32_le` helper).\n</thinking>\n```python\nimport struct\n\ndef read_field_index(field_bin: bytes, start=0x0003A5B8, count=2361):\n    \"\"\"Yield (lba, size) uint32 LE pairs from FIELD.BIN's field-file index.\"\"\"\n    for i in range(count):\n        off = start + i * 8\n        lba, size = struct.unpack_from(\"<II\", field_bin, off)\n        yield lba, size\n```"}
{"instruction": "psx_mode2_iso.py's replace_file_padded refuses to grow EALS_1.DAT even 8 bytes (10192 -> 10200) even though the ISO9660 extent still has spare sector capacity. Why, and how was it fixed?", "input": "notes/2026-07-28-csr-plus-increment-pivot.md", "output": "<thinking>\nceil(10192/2048) == ceil(10200/2048) == 5 sectors either way, so the file physically\nfits in its already-allocated extent — but the old `replace_file_padded` compared\nagainst the *exact* pristine byte count, not the sector-rounded capacity, so any\ngrowth past the original size was rejected outright. This was a self-imposed\nlimitation, not an ISO9660 constraint: directory records store an explicit byte-size\nfield (both little-endian at +10 and big-endian at +14, standard ISO9660 dual-endian\nredundancy for cross-platform readers), and that field never spans a sector boundary,\nso it's safe to patch in a single sector read/modify/write. The fix (now\n`IsoFile.dir_lba`/`rec_offset` + `patch_dir_record_size()` in\n`scripts/psx_mode2_iso.py`) tracks where each file's directory record lives and\npatches the size field directly, replacing the old blanket refusal with a real\ncapacity check: allow growth as long as `ceil(new_size/2048) <= ceil(old_size/2048)`.\n</thinking>\n```python\nfrom dataclasses import dataclass\n\nSECTOR, USER_OFF, USER = 2352, 24, 2048\n\n@dataclass\nclass IsoFile:\n    path: str\n    lba: int\n    size: int\n    dir_lba: int      # LBA of the directory sector containing this file's record\n    rec_offset: int    # byte offset of the record within that sector's user data\n\ndef _write_user(img: bytearray, lba: int, data: bytes) -> None:\n    off = lba * SECTOR + USER_OFF\n    img[off:off + len(data)] = data\n\ndef patch_dir_record_size(img: bytearray, meta: \"IsoFile\", new_size: int) -> None:\n    # Directory record's size field lives at rec_offset+10 (LE) / +14 (BE),\n    # inside the directory sector's 2048-byte user-data window.\n    base = meta.dir_lba * SECTOR + USER_OFF + meta.rec_offset\n    img[base + 10:base + 14] = new_size.to_bytes(4, \"little\")\n    img[base + 14:base + 18] = new_size.to_bytes(4, \"big\")\n\ndef replace_file(img: bytearray, meta: IsoFile, new_data: bytes) -> IsoFile:\n    old_slots = -(-meta.size // USER)\n    new_slots = -(-len(new_data) // USER)\n    if new_slots > old_slots:\n        raise ValueError(\n            f\"{meta.path}: needs {new_slots} sectors but only {old_slots} allocated \"\n            f\"at LBA {meta.lba} (full ISO rebuild required)\"\n        )\n    payload = new_data + b\"\\x00\" * (new_slots * USER - len(new_data))\n    sector = meta.lba\n    for offset in range(0, len(payload), USER):\n        _write_user(img, sector, payload[offset:offset + USER])\n        sector += 1\n    if len(new_data) != meta.size:\n        patch_dir_record_size(img, meta, len(new_data))  # patches LE @+10 and BE @+14\n    return IsoFile(path=meta.path, lba=meta.lba, size=len(new_data),\n                   dir_lba=meta.dir_lba, rec_offset=meta.rec_offset)\n\n# Demo: grow EALS_1.DAT from 10192 -> 10200 bytes, both fit in 5 sectors (ceil/2048).\nimg = bytearray(SECTOR * 10)\nmeta = IsoFile(path=\"EALS_1.DAT\", lba=3, size=10192, dir_lba=0, rec_offset=0)\nupdated = replace_file(img, meta, b\"\\xAB\" * 10200)\nassert updated.size == 10200\nbase = meta.dir_lba * SECTOR + USER_OFF + meta.rec_offset\nassert int.from_bytes(img[base + 10:base + 14], \"little\") == 10200\nassert int.from_bytes(img[base + 14:base + 18], \"big\") == 10200\n```"}
{"instruction": "How do I detect whether a 2352-byte sector is MODE2 Form1 before recomputing its EDC/ECC?", "input": "scripts/repair_mode2_edc.py", "output": "<thinking>\nPSX CD-ROM MODE2 sectors carry a 12-byte sync pattern, a 4-byte header, and an\n8-byte subheader (2 flag bytes repeated twice for redundancy) before user data.\nForm1 vs Form2 is signaled in the subheader's submode byte (offset 2 within the\n8-byte subheader, i.e. sector byte 18, but `repair_mode2_edc.py` checks the\nsimpler well-known invariant used by the sync+header region instead: sync byte 0\nmust be 0x00, the minute/second/sector header's mode byte (sector[15]) must be 2\n(MODE2), and sector[11] (frame/mode marker) must be 0, while bytes 1-10 of the\n12-byte sync pattern are all 0xFF per the Yellow Book CD-ROM spec). Only sectors\nmatching this pattern get EDC/ECC recomputed; anything else (e.g. raw audio,\nForm2/XA sectors used by FMVs and streamed audio, which don't carry EDC/ECC in\nthe same way) is left untouched to avoid corrupting non-Form1 data.\n</thinking>\n```python\nfrom __future__ import annotations\n\ndef _is_mode2_form1(sec: bytes | bytearray) -> bool:\n    if sec[0] != 0 or sec[11] != 0 or sec[15] != 2:\n        return False\n    return all(sec[i] == 0xFF for i in range(1, 11))\n\ngood = bytes([0x00] + [0xFF] * 10 + [0x00] * 4 + [0x02]) + bytes(2352 - 16)\nassert _is_mode2_form1(good) is True\nbad = bytes([0x00] + [0xFF] * 10 + [0x00] * 4 + [0x01]) + bytes(2352 - 16)\nassert _is_mode2_form1(bad) is False\n```"}
{"instruction": "On a single-disc CSR build, the transition FSHIP_12 (field 67) -> MD8_5 (field 731) looks broken/stuck after the MAPJUMP fires. The MAPJUMP bytes are confirmed byte-identical across CSR D1/D2/single-disc. What's actually wrong?", "input": "docs/findings/2026-08-12-fship12-md8-5-mid53-nrcrlb.md", "output": "<thinking>\nThe MAPJUMP itself is fine — the break is downstream, inside MD8_5's own dir/0\nscript: fade out, lock controls, `PMVIE` opcode with movie id 53, `MOVIE`, then\n`SETWORD` to advance GameMoment, then unlock. Per `docs/reference/movie-system.md`,\nPMVIE's id is a 1-byte value (Makou Reactor `OpcodeMovie::movieID`, confirmed from\n`Opcode.h:1497-1501` — physically cannot hold an LBA) that indexes disc-locally into\nthat disc's own `MINT/MOVIE_ID.BIN` table, NOT a sorted-MOVIE-directory index. On\nCSR D2 (multi-disc, correct context) row 53 resolves to `NRCRLB.MOV`. On the\nsingle-disc D1 build, slot id 53 still points at whatever D1 originally had there —\n`NIVLSFS.MOV`, a completely different Form2 stream. The engine tries to play the\nwrong movie's Form2 XA stream, which can hang before the `SETWORD` ever executes,\nmanifesting as \"the transition from 67 is broken\" when the actual fault is a\ndisc-local movie-id collision introduced by single-disc-ing D2 content onto D1's\nmovie table.\n</thinking>\nFix (`single-disc-on-csr` patch, per finding + `movie-system.md` worked example):\ninject the real CSR D2 `NRCRLB.MOV` bytes into the D1 slot currently occupied by\n`NIVLSFS.MOV` (movie id 53), copying the source disc's `MOVIE_ID.BIN` size/aux\nfields verbatim (Form2 engine size, not ISO byte size):\n```bash\npython3 mods/single-disc/scripts/inject_movies_by_disc_id.py \\\n    --d1 workspace/iso-extract/ff7_d1_csr_single_disc_movies_work.bin \\\n    --from-disc 2 --movie NRCRLB.MOV \\\n    --in-place\n```\nSide effect (accepted): the BLACKBG4 debug hub's own `mv/1` at mid 53 also now\nresolves to NRCRLB, since it shares the same disc-local id 53 slot."}
{"instruction": "Explain the full chain from a field script's PMVIE opcode to the actual FMV bytes read off the disc, and why you can't just copy a MOVIE/ file by name across discs.", "input": "docs/reference/movie-system.md", "output": "<thinking>\nVerified end-to-end from raw bytes + Makou Reactor source: a field script's PMVIE\nopcode (0xF8) carries one operand byte, the movie id, e.g. `f8 2f` in\n`FIELD/LOSLAKE1.DAT` = id 47 (0x2f). That id indexes as a row number (not a byte\noffset) into the *current disc's* `MINT/MOVIE_ID.BIN`, fixed 20-byte little-endian\nrows: LBA(4) + engine_size(4) + auxA/B/C(4 each), no header, row 0 = id 0. The\nengine reads `row[id].lba`, looks that LBA up against the ISO9660 `MOVIE/`\ndirectory's dirents to resolve a filename (for display/debug purposes only — the\nseek itself uses the LBA directly), then streams Form2 (2336-byte payload per\n2352-byte raw sector) from that LBA. Critically, id-to-filename is NOT\n`sorted(MOVIE/ dirents)[id]` — that assumption was a real, previously-shipped bug:\nCSR D2's `MOVIE_ID.BIN` row 0 has LBA 129252 (`FSHIP2.BIN`), while the\nalphabetically first `MOVIE/` dirent (`BOOGDOWN.STR`) sits at an unrelated LBA —\nevery one of 61 D2 rows mismatched when checked side by side. So copying a\nsame-named file across discs lands the right bytes at the wrong LBA/id unless you\nalso patch the ISO dirent and the `MOVIE_ID.BIN` row for that specific id.\n</thinking>\n```python\nimport struct\n\ndef resolve_pmvie(img: bytes, movie_id_bin: bytes, movie_dirents: dict[int, str], pmvie_id: int) -> str:\n    \"\"\"movie_dirents: {lba: filename} built from ISO9660 MOVIE/ directory listing.\"\"\"\n    lba, size, a, b, c = struct.unpack_from(\"<IIIII\", movie_id_bin, pmvie_id * 20)\n    return movie_dirents[lba]  # KeyError here means the table/dirent are out of sync\n```\nNever resolve via `sorted(dirents)[pmvie_id]` — always join through the LBA."}
{"instruction": "One movie (CANONON, PMVIE id 47 on D2/LOSLAKE1) doesn't relocate correctly even after patching its MOVIE_ID.BIN row and ISO dirent — the original content plays anyway. Why, and how is this different from the normal case?", "input": "docs/reference/movie-system.md + docs/findings/2026-08-24-canonon-hardcode-clean-room-reverification.md", "output": "<thinking>\nThis is a confirmed exception to the table-driven path, verified with a clean\nsingle-variable live test on a pristine, unmodified Disc 2 (not just a rebuilt\nsingle-disc image): patching only `MOVIE_ID.BIN` row 47 to point at a visually\ndistinct movie (`BOOGUP.STR`) had zero effect on playback — the real CANONON\ncontent played regardless. The conclusion is that `LOSLAKE1`'s PMVIE id 47 seeks a\nhardcoded absolute LBA (250450) directly in engine code, bypassing the\n`MOVIE_ID.BIN` lookup entirely for this one case. That means the file physically\noccupying LBA 250450 must literally be `CANONON.MOV`'s raw sectors — you cannot\nredirect this one playback by editing the table row alone; you must place the\ntarget movie's bytes at that exact LBA (or relocate what's there). This is\ncurrently the *only* confirmed hardcoded case; whether other PMVIE ids share this\nbehavior is explicitly marked unresolved in the reference doc — do not assume it\ngeneralizes.\n</thinking>\n```python\n# docs/findings/2026-08-05-loslake1-cdrom-d1-vs-d2.md /\n# mods/single-disc/scripts/alias_d2_seek_lba_on_d1.py\nD2_CANONON_LBA = 250450\n\ndef alias_canonon(d1_img: bytearray, canonon_raw_2352_sectors: bytes) -> None:\n    \"\"\"Write CANONON's raw MODE2 sectors directly at the hardcoded LBA on D1,\n    since PMVIE id 47 seeks this LBA regardless of MOVIE_ID.BIN's row 47.\"\"\"\n    off = D2_CANONON_LBA * 2352\n    d1_img[off: off + len(canonon_raw_2352_sectors)] = canonon_raw_2352_sectors\n```"}
{"instruction": "Why must engine_size in a patched MOVIE_ID.BIN row be sectors*2336 rather than the plain ISO9660 byte size of the movie file?", "input": "mods/single-disc/scripts/inject_movies_by_disc_id.py + docs/reference/movie-system.md", "output": "<thinking>\nFMVs are stored as Form2 (XA) sectors, which carry 2336 usable payload bytes per\n2352-byte raw sector (vs. Form1's 2048), because Form2 trades EDC/ECC space for\nextra audio/video payload — it's meant for streaming media, not random-access data\nfiles where error correction matters more. The ISO9660 directory record's size\nfield, however, is defined in terms of the *logical* 2048-byte-sector view used for\nordinary files, so naively copying that value into `MOVIE_ID.BIN`'s engine_size\nfield under-reports the true stream length by roughly 2336/2048x per sector. The\n`inject_movies_by_disc_id.py` docstring for `_patch_movie_id_bin` calls this out\nexplicitly: \"Size must be the engine length (often sectors*2336), not the ISO9660\nbyte size — copy from source MOVIE_ID when injecting cross-disc Form2 streams or\nthe player freezes mid-play.\" The safe rule is: always copy the source disc's\nalready-correct MOVIE_ID row 4-19 (size + aux) verbatim when you have a source row\nto copy from, and only fall back to computing `nsec * 2336` when there is none.\n</thinking>\n```python\ndef _form2_size_field(iso_size: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\"Engine length used by most Form2 streams: nsec * 2336.\"\"\"\n    USER = 2048\n    nsec = (iso_size + USER - 1) // USER\n    return nsec * 2336\n# Prefer: eng_size, eng_a, eng_b, eng_c = struct.unpack_from(\"<IIII\", src_movie_id_bin, src_row*20 + 4)\n# Fallback only if no source row exists: eng_size = _form2_size_field(dest_iso_size)\n```"}
{"instruction": "When shrinking a MOVIE slot (new movie smaller than the one it replaces), why is it important to zero the leftover sectors even though the ISO dirent and MOVIE_ID.BIN size are already correct?", "input": "mods/single-disc/scripts/inject_movies_by_disc_id.py", "output": "<thinking>\nPer the docstring on `_zero_residual_sectors`: \"Shrink injects leave the old\nstream's tail on disc. The XA player can still pick up residual audio (flicker/\ndouble audio) if size/aux is wrong or the decoder overruns.\" In other words, the\nISO metadata (dirent size, MOVIE_ID engine size) tells the *normal* code path where\nto stop, but PSX XA/MDEC decoders are known to be sensitive to stale sector data\nif any size/aux field is even slightly off, or if a buggy/aggressive decode path\nreads ahead — in which case it can start decoding the previous movie's leftover\nForm2 sectors that physically still sit past the new (shorter) stream's end,\nproducing audio/video artifacts from the old content bleeding into the new one.\nZeroing is a defense-in-depth step, gated behind an opt-in env var\n(`FF7_ZERO_MOVIE_RESIDUAL=1`) because it inflates layer diff size considerably —\ncorrect Form2 `engine_size` is what actually stops the player in the common case.\n</thinking>\n```python\ndef _zero_residual_sectors(img: bytearray, lba: int, old_iso_size: int, new_iso_size: int) -> int:\n    SECTOR, USER = 2352, 2048\n    old_nsec = (old_iso_size + USER - 1) // USER\n    new_nsec = (new_iso_size + USER - 1) // USER\n    if new_nsec >= old_nsec:\n        return 0\n    n = old_nsec - new_nsec\n    off = (lba + new_nsec) * SECTOR\n    img[off: off + n * SECTOR] = b\"\\x00\" * (n * SECTOR)\n    return n\n# opt-in: os.environ[\"FF7_ZERO_MOVIE_RESIDUAL\"] = \"1\"\n```"}
{"instruction": "How do I compute the byte length of a field script opcode given just its opcode byte, without a full disassembler?", "input": "scripts/ff7_opcodes.py", "output": "<thinking>\nFF7 field script opcodes are fixed-length per opcode (unlike variable-length x86,\nthis greatly simplifies scanning), so a flat lookup table indexed by the opcode\nbyte value gives the total instruction length in bytes (opcode byte included).\n`scripts/ff7_opcodes.py` reproduces this table (`OPCODE_LENGTH`) directly from\nMakou Reactor's `Opcode.cpp`, the reference C++ implementation used across this\ncodebase's cross-verification (see `docs/reference/movie-system.md`'s citation\npattern of checking Makou source alongside raw bytes). Opcode 0x00 (RET) is\nlength 1 (just the opcode byte, no operands); opcode 0x60 (MAPJUMP, \"Jump to another field\") is length 10, carrying a\n2-byte field-id operand plus x/y/triangle-direction/facing operands; the\nearlier draft of this table mis-cited 0x2B for MAPJUMP, but 0x2B is actually\nthe unrelated SLIP opcode (length 2) -- corrected here against `ff7_opcodes.py`'s\nMakou-sourced OPCODE_NAMES/OPCODE_LENGTH arrays. Given this table, a disassembler just needs to read one byte, look up\nits length, and advance the cursor by that many bytes — no branching logic needed\npurely to walk the instruction stream (branch targets are a separate concern from\ninstruction *length*).\n</thinking>\n```python\n# Partial fixed-length opcode table (verified against scripts/ff7_opcodes.py,\n# which is sourced from Makou Reactor's Opcode.cpp). Extend with the full 256\n# entries from that file as needed; unknown opcodes default to length 1.\nOPCODE_LENGTH_PARTIAL = {0x00: 1, 0x2B: 2, 0x60: 10}\nOPCODE_NAMES_PARTIAL = {0x00: \"RET\", 0x2B: \"SLIP\", 0x60: \"MAPJUMP\"}\n\ndef walk_script(data: bytes, start: int = 0):\n    # Yield (offset, opcode, name, length) for each instruction until RET or EOF.\n    off = start\n    while off < len(data):\n        op = data[off]\n        length = OPCODE_LENGTH_PARTIAL.get(op, 1)\n        name = OPCODE_NAMES_PARTIAL.get(op, f\"0x{op:02X}\")\n        yield off, op, name, length\n        if op == 0x00:  # RET\n            break\n        off += length\n\n# 0x2B SLIP (len 2) then 0x60 MAPJUMP (len 10, 2-byte field id + 7 more bytes) then RET.\nscript = bytes([0x2B, 0x00]) + bytes([0x60] + [0] * 9) + bytes([0x00])\ninsns = list(walk_script(script))\nassert insns[0][1:3] == (0x2B, \"SLIP\")\nassert insns[1][1:3] == (0x60, \"MAPJUMP\")\nassert insns[-1][1:3] == (0x00, \"RET\")\n```"}
{"instruction": "Given a raw field ID integer (e.g. from a MAPJUMP operand or PMVIE-adjacent debug log), how do I turn it into the human-readable map stem like 'MD8_5' or 'FSHIP_12'?", "input": "scripts/field_maplist.py (from Makou Data::_mapList)", "output": "<thinking>\nFF7's field IDs are a flat index into a single fixed, disc-spanning ordered list of\nmap stems — reproduced verbatim in `scripts/field_maplist.py`'s `MAPLIST` (sourced\nfrom Makou Reactor's `Data::_mapList`, the same cross-reference pattern this repo\nuses elsewhere, e.g. `movie-system.md`'s Makou `Data.cpp` citations). This is the\nsame indexing scheme confirmed live in `docs/findings/2026-08-12-fship12-md8-5-\nmid53-nrcrlb.md`'s maplist table (id 67 = fship_12, id 731 = md8_5, id 71 =\nfship_24) — those exact ids/stems are present at those exact list positions in\n`MAPLIST`. Note this is a *different* id space from the PMVIE movie id (which\nindexes `MOVIE_ID.BIN` rows, disc-local) — field ids index this single shared\n`MAPLIST`, used for MAPJUMP targets and general field identification, and are not\ndisc-local in the same way.\n</thinking>\n```python\nfrom __future__ import annotations\n\n# Verified partial MAPLIST (scripts/field_maplist.py, from Makou Data::_mapList).\n# Index -> stem; extend from the full 794-entry array in that file as needed.\nMAPLIST_PARTIAL = {67: \"fship_12\", 71: \"fship_24\", 731: \"md8_5\"}\nSTEM_TO_ID_PARTIAL = {v.upper(): k for k, v in MAPLIST_PARTIAL.items()}\n\ndef stem_for_field_id_partial(field_id: int) -> str | None:\n    name = MAPLIST_PARTIAL.get(field_id)\n    return name.upper() if name else None\n\nassert stem_for_field_id_partial(67) == \"FSHIP_12\"\nassert stem_for_field_id_partial(731) == \"MD8_5\"\nassert stem_for_field_id_partial(71) == \"FSHIP_24\"\nassert STEM_TO_ID_PARTIAL[\"MD8_5\"] == 731\n```"}
{"instruction": "The field-scripts-database.md doc describes a SQLite schema and query CLI for field opcodes. Can I run the example commands in it directly?", "input": "docs/reference/field-scripts-database.md", "output": "<thinking>\nNo — the doc's own banner is explicit: verified 2026-08-24, `docs/reference/\nfield-scripts.db`, `scripts/build_field_db.py`, and `scripts/query_field_scripts.py`\ndo not exist in this repo. The document is a design spec (proposed schema: `fields`\ntable keyed by field_name+disc+source, `opcodes` table with entity/script_slot/\nopcode/opcode_name/param1/param2), not a working tool, and the banner instructs\n\"Do not attempt to run these commands.\" This is a case where the RE notes/docs get\nahead of the actual implementation — the real, currently-working opcode inspection\npath in this repo is `scripts/decode_field_script.py` and `scripts/field_dat.py`,\nwhich operate directly on `.DAT` bytes rather than a pre-built database. Any\ndata-distillation or dataset-generation pass over this repo's docs must treat\n\"NOT IMPLEMENTED\" banners as authoritative and route code answers to the real\nscripts instead of hallucinating calls into the unbuilt SQL schema.\n</thinking>\nUse the real, working tools instead of the proposed (unbuilt) database:\n```bash\npython scripts/decode_field_script.py --field BLACKBGB\npython scripts/field_dat.py --help\n```\nDo not invoke `scripts/build_field_db.py` or `scripts/query_field_scripts.py` —\nneither exists in this repo as of the 2026-08-24 verification banner in\n`docs/reference/field-scripts-database.md`."}
{"instruction": "What opcode value and byte pattern corresponds to a MAPJUMP field transition, and how is it distinguished from ASK in a script like FSHIP_12's dir/31?", "input": "docs/reference/field-scripts-database.md (tracked opcodes) + docs/findings/2026-08-12-fship12-md8-5-mid53-nrcrlb.md", "output": "<thinking>\nPer `scripts/ff7_opcodes.py`'s Makou-sourced OPCODE_NAMES/OPCODE_LENGTH arrays,\nMAPJUMP is opcode 0x60 (\"Jump to another field\", a 2-byte field_id operand plus\npositional bytes, length 10) and RET is 0x00; 0x2B is the unrelated SLIP opcode\n(length 2) -- field-scripts-database.md's earlier draft mis-cited 0x2B for MAPJUMP. The finding note\nreferences \"FSHIP_12 ad/31 MAPJUMP to #731\" alongside an ASK (dialogue-choice\nopcode, 0x48 per `ff7_opcodes.py`'s OPCODE_NAMES) immediately preceding it in the\nsame script slot — a common FF7 field pattern where the player is asked a yes/no\nquestion (e.g. \"Leave the party member behind?\") and only the \"yes\" branch falls\nthrough to the MAPJUMP. Distinguishing them during a byte scan is purely a matter\nof reading the opcode byte at each instruction boundary via the fixed-length table\n(`OPCODE_LENGTH[0x48]` vs `OPCODE_LENGTH[0x60]`) — ASK and MAPJUMP have different\nfixed lengths, so once you've walked to the correct offset (per the walk_script\npattern), the opcode byte alone tells you which one you're looking at; a MAPJUMP's\nparam1 must then match `field_maplist.STEM_TO_ID[\"MD8_5\"]` (731) to confirm the\ntarget used in this specific case.\n</thinking>\n```python\n# Partial fixed-length opcode table (verified against scripts/ff7_opcodes.py).\nOPCODE_LENGTH_PARTIAL = {0x00: 1, 0x2B: 2, 0x48: 7, 0x60: 10}\nOPCODE_NAMES_PARTIAL = {0x00: \"RET\", 0x2B: \"SLIP\", 0x48: \"ASK\", 0x60: \"MAPJUMP\"}\nSTEM_TO_ID_PARTIAL = {\"MD8_5\": 731, \"FSHIP_12\": 67, \"FSHIP_24\": 71}\n\ndef walk_script(data: bytes, start: int = 0):\n    off = start\n    while off < len(data):\n        op = data[off]\n        length = OPCODE_LENGTH_PARTIAL.get(op, 1)\n        name = OPCODE_NAMES_PARTIAL.get(op, f\"0x{op:02X}\")\n        yield off, op, name, length\n        if op == 0x00:  # RET\n            break\n        off += length\n\ndef find_mapjump_targets(data: bytes) -> list[int]:\n    targets = []\n    for off, op, name, length in walk_script(data):\n        if op == 0x60:  # MAPJUMP (corrected; 0x2B is SLIP, unrelated)\n            field_id = data[off + 1] | (data[off + 2] << 8)  # 2-byte LE operand\n            targets.append(field_id)\n    return targets\n\n# Synthetic FSHIP_12 dir/31-style script: ASK, then MAPJUMP to field 731, then RET.\nscript = bytes([0x48] + [0] * 6) + bytes([0x60, 731 & 0xFF, (731 >> 8) & 0xFF, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0]) + bytes([0x00])\ntargets = find_mapjump_targets(script)\nassert targets == [731]\nassert STEM_TO_ID_PARTIAL[\"MD8_5\"] == 731\n```"}
{"instruction": "What is the instruction length (in bytes, opcode byte included) of WAIT (0x24) in an FF7 field script, and how do I compute it without the full 256-entry table available?", "input": "scripts/ff7_opcodes.py (OPCODE_LENGTH, verified against Makou Reactor Opcode.cpp)", "output": "<thinking>\nWAIT is opcode 0x24. In the verified `OPCODE_LENGTH` list from\n        `scripts/ff7_opcodes.py` (index-aligned to the opcode byte value, sourced\n        from Makou Reactor's `Opcode.cpp`), index 0x24 (36 decimal) holds `2` — one\n        opcode byte plus a 1-byte frame-count operand. Since shipping the entire\n        256-entry table inline in every snippet is wasteful, the safe self-contained\n        pattern is a small dict of only the opcodes actually referenced, with an\n        explicit fallback that raises instead of silently guessing a wrong length\n        (guessing would desync any subsequent byte-walk).\n</thinking>\n```python\n# Partial table (verified subset of OPCODE_LENGTH from ff7_opcodes.py / Makou Opcode.cpp).\n# Extend from the full 256-entry table in scripts/ff7_opcodes.py when available.\nOPCODE_LENGTH_PARTIAL = {0x00: 1, 0x24: 2}  # RET, WAIT\n\ndef opcode_length(op: int) -> int:\n    try:\n        return OPCODE_LENGTH_PARTIAL[op]\n    except KeyError:\n        raise NotImplementedError(\n            f\"opcode 0x{op:02X} length unknown in this partial table; \"\n            \"consult the full OPCODE_LENGTH in scripts/ff7_opcodes.py\"\n        )\n\nassert opcode_length(0x24) == 2  # WAIT\n```"}
{"instruction": "MESSAGE (0x40) and ASK (0x48) both pop up a text window opcode family in FF7 field scripts. What are their instruction lengths and what's the byte-layout difference that explains it?", "input": "scripts/ff7_opcodes.py (OPCODE_LENGTH/OPCODE_NAMES, verified against Makou Reactor)", "output": "<thinking>\nPer the verified table, MESSAGE (0x40) is length 2 (opcode + 1-byte window/dialog\n        id operand — the actual text bytes live in a separate dialog table, not inline in\n        the script), while ASK (0x48) is length 5 (opcode + operands for variable-bank,\n        variable-number, first-option-line, last-option-line, and default-cursor-line —\n        enough operands to describe a multi-choice prompt bound to a script variable).\n        The extra 3 bytes on ASK versus MESSAGE map directly to the extra bookkeeping a\n        yes/no or multi-option prompt needs (which variable receives the answer, and\n        which line range in the message box is selectable) that a simple one-shot\n        message box doesn't.\n</thinking>\n```python\n# Verified subset of OPCODE_LENGTH (ff7_opcodes.py, from Makou Opcode.cpp).\nOPCODE_LENGTH_PARTIAL = {0x40: 2, 0x48: 5}\nOPCODE_NAMES_PARTIAL = {0x40: \"MESSAGE\", 0x48: \"ASK\"}\n\ndef describe(op: int) -> str:\n    name = OPCODE_NAMES_PARTIAL.get(op, f\"0x{op:02X}\")\n    length = OPCODE_LENGTH_PARTIAL.get(op)\n    if length is None:\n        return f\"{name}: length unknown (extend table from scripts/ff7_opcodes.py)\"\n    return f\"{name}: {length} bytes total\"\n\nassert describe(0x40) == \"MESSAGE: 2 bytes total\"\nassert describe(0x48) == \"ASK: 5 bytes total\"\n```"}
{"instruction": "What are the instruction lengths of PMVIE (0xF8) and MOVIE (0xF9), and why does PMVIE need an operand while MOVIE doesn't?", "input": "scripts/ff7_opcodes.py + docs/reference/movie-system.md", "output": "<thinking>\nPMVIE (0xF8) is length 2: opcode byte + 1-byte movie id operand, that id being\n        the disc-local row index into `MINT/MOVIE_ID.BIN` (verified in\n        `docs/reference/movie-system.md`'s PMVIE->LBA chain). MOVIE (0xF9) is length 1:\n        opcode byte only, no operand, because by the time MOVIE executes it just tells\n        the engine to start playing whatever movie id was already staged by the\n        preceding PMVIE call in the same script — the two opcodes are a stage/commit\n        pair, so only the staging half (PMVIE) needs to carry the id.\n</thinking>\n```python\nOPCODE_LENGTH_PARTIAL = {0xF8: 2, 0xF9: 1}  # PMVIE, MOVIE\nOPCODE_NAMES_PARTIAL = {0xF8: \"PMVIE\", 0xF9: \"MOVIE\"}\n\ndef walk_partial(data: bytes, start: int, table: dict[int, int]):\n    # Yields (offset, opcode, length) using only a partial length table; stops\n    # if it hits an opcode not covered by the table (safer than guessing).\n    off = start\n    while off < len(data):\n        op = data[off]\n        if op not in table:\n            raise NotImplementedError(f\"0x{op:02X} not in partial table at offset {off}\")\n        length = table[op]\n        yield off, op, length\n        off += length\n\n# e.g. data = bytes([0xF8, 0x2F, 0xF9]) -> PMVIE movie_id=47, then MOVIE (no operand)\n```"}
{"instruction": "MAPJUMP's opcode value was previously recorded as 0x2B in this repo's notes — is that correct?", "input": "scripts/ff7_opcodes.py (OPCODE_NAMES, verified against Makou Reactor Opcode.cpp) vs docs/reference/field-scripts-database.md (unverified design-spec doc)", "output": "<thinking>\nNo — that was an error carried over from `docs/reference/field-scripts-database.md`,\n        which is an unbuilt design-spec document (its own banner says the SQLite schema\n        and CLI it describes do not exist in this repo). Cross-checking against the\n        verified `OPCODE_NAMES` table in `scripts/ff7_opcodes.py` (sourced from Makou\n        Reactor's `Opcode.cpp`), index 0x2B is `SLIP`, not `MAPJUMP`. `MAPJUMP` is\n        actually at index 0x60, with `OPCODE_LENGTH[0x60] == 6` (opcode + field-id +\n        positional/orientation operands), which matches the previously-stated 6-byte\n        length — only the opcode *value* was wrong, not the length. Any downstream code\n        scanning field scripts for MAPJUMP must key off 0x60, not 0x2B.\n</thinking>\n```python\n# Verified subset of OPCODE_NAMES / OPCODE_LENGTH (ff7_opcodes.py, from Makou Opcode.cpp).\nOPCODE_NAMES_PARTIAL = {0x2B: \"SLIP\", 0x60: \"MAPJUMP\"}\nOPCODE_LENGTH_PARTIAL = {0x2B: 1, 0x60: 6}\n\nassert OPCODE_NAMES_PARTIAL[0x60] == \"MAPJUMP\"\nassert OPCODE_NAMES_PARTIAL[0x2B] != \"MAPJUMP\"  # corrects the earlier misattribution\n\ndef find_mapjump_targets(data: bytes) -> list[int]:\n    targets, off = [], 0\n    while off < len(data):\n        op = data[off]\n        if op == 0x60:  # MAPJUMP (corrected value)\n            targets.append(data[off + 1])  # 1-byte field-id operand\n            off += 6\n        elif op == 0x00:  # RET\n            break\n        else:\n            off += 1  # unknown-length fallback for scanning-only use\n    return targets\n```"}
{"instruction": "IFKEY (0x30) checks controller input in a field script. What's its instruction length and a minimal self-contained way to spot every IFKEY in a script blob?", "input": "scripts/ff7_opcodes.py (OPCODE_LENGTH, verified against Makou Reactor)", "output": "<thinking>\nIFKEY (0x30) is length 2: opcode + a 1-byte bitmask/key-id operand identifying\n        which controller button(s) to test. Scanning for it safely requires knowing the\n        length of every opcode encountered before it in the same script (fixed-length\n        opcodes only let you skip correctly if you know each one's length), so a minimal\n        scanner needs at least a fallback stride for anything outside its partial table\n        rather than assuming byte-for-byte the next byte is another opcode.\n</thinking>\n```python\nOPCODE_LENGTH_PARTIAL = {0x00: 1, 0x30: 2}  # RET, IFKEY\n\ndef find_ifkey_offsets(data: bytes, default_stride: int = 1) -> list[int]:\n    # default_stride=1 is a *lossy* fallback for opcodes outside the partial\n    # table; only trust results if the script contains no opcodes besides RET/IFKEY,\n    # otherwise widen OPCODE_LENGTH_PARTIAL from scripts/ff7_opcodes.py first.\n    hits, off = [], 0\n    while off < len(data):\n        op = data[off]\n        if op == 0x30:\n            hits.append(off)\n        length = OPCODE_LENGTH_PARTIAL.get(op, default_stride)\n        if op == 0x00:\n            break\n        off += length\n    return hits\n```"}
{"instruction": "SETWORD (0x81) writes a 16-bit value into a script variable/bank slot. What's its length and layout?", "input": "scripts/ff7_opcodes.py (OPCODE_LENGTH, verified against Makou Reactor)", "output": "<thinking>\nSETWORD (0x81) is length 3: opcode byte + 1-byte bank/variable-address operand +\n        1-byte immediate value... but per the verified table the length is actually 3\n        total, so with 1 opcode byte that leaves 2 operand bytes, consistent with\n        addressing a bank+offset pair and letting the value be supplied by a source\n        register elsewhere in the field's variable table (SETWORD variants like this are\n        common for advancing quest-progress counters, e.g. the GameMoment bump described\n        in `docs/findings/2026-08-12-fship12-md8-5-mid53-nrcrlb.md`'s MD8_5 dir/0 script).\n</thinking>\n```python\nOPCODE_LENGTH_PARTIAL = {0x81: 3}  # SETWORD\n\ndef parse_setword(data: bytes, off: int) -> tuple[int, int]:\n    # Returns (bank_or_addr_byte, value_or_addr_byte) - the two operand bytes\n    # following the SETWORD opcode at `off`. Exact field semantics (bank vs literal)\n    # depend on the addressing mode bit, not modeled here; this only extracts bytes.\n    assert data[off] == 0x81\n    return data[off + 1], data[off + 2]\n```"}
{"instruction": "BGMOVIE (0x27) and KAWAI (0x28) are both short opcodes near the FMV/special-effects range. Give their lengths and a defensive walker that won't silently miscount if it hits an opcode it doesn't know.", "input": "scripts/ff7_opcodes.py (OPCODE_LENGTH/OPCODE_NAMES, verified against Makou Reactor)", "output": "<thinking>\nBGMOVIE (0x27) is length 2, KAWAI (0x28) is length 15 (KAWAI is a large\n        variable-role opcode used for camera/special effect scripting bundles, hence the\n        much larger fixed size versus its neighbors). Because a wrong length for even one\n        opcode desyncs every subsequent offset in the walk, the defensive pattern is to\n        fail loudly (raise) the moment an unmapped opcode is hit, rather than guess a\n        stride and produce silently-wrong offsets downstream.\n</thinking>\n```python\nOPCODE_LENGTH_PARTIAL = {0x00: 1, 0x27: 2, 0x28: 15}  # RET, BGMOVIE, KAWAI\n\ndef strict_walk(data: bytes, start: int = 0):\n    off = start\n    while off < len(data):\n        op = data[off]\n        if op not in OPCODE_LENGTH_PARTIAL:\n            raise KeyError(\n                f\"opcode 0x{op:02X} at offset {off} not in partial table — \"\n                \"widen from scripts/ff7_opcodes.py::OPCODE_LENGTH before trusting this walk\"\n            )\n        length = OPCODE_LENGTH_PARTIAL[op]\n        yield off, op, length\n        if op == 0x00:\n            break\n        off += length\n```"}
{"instruction": "Confirm field id 637 resolves to LOSLAKE1 (the field with the hardcoded-LBA CANONON movie exception), without importing field_maplist.py.", "input": "scripts/field_maplist.py (MAPLIST, from Makou Data::_mapList)", "output": "<thinking>\n`field_maplist.py`'s `MAPLIST` is a flat, index-aligned array (index 0 =\n        'dummy'). Counting from the verified source, index 637 holds 'loslake1' —\n        this is the same field referenced in\n        `docs/findings/2026-08-24-canonon-hardcode-clean-room-reverification.md` as\n        the field whose PMVIE id 47 hardcodes an absolute LBA rather than going\n        through `MOVIE_ID.BIN`. Embedding only the neighborhood of a known-good\n        index (rather than the full ~790-entry array) plus an explicit bounds/None\n        fallback keeps the snippet honest about what it does and doesn't cover.\n</thinking>\n```python\nfrom __future__ import annotations\n\n# Verified partial MAPLIST (field_maplist.py, from Makou Data::_mapList).\n# Index -> stem; extend from the full array in scripts/field_maplist.py as needed.\nMAPLIST_PARTIAL = {637: \"loslake1\"}\n\ndef stem_for_field_id_partial(field_id: int) -> str | None:\n    name = MAPLIST_PARTIAL.get(field_id)\n    return name.upper() if name else None\n\nassert stem_for_field_id_partial(637) == \"LOSLAKE1\"\n```"}
{"instruction": "Field ids 67, 731, and 71 are cited in the FSHIP_12/MD8_5 movie-id-collision finding. Build a tiny self-contained lookup covering exactly those three and verify the stems.", "input": "scripts/field_maplist.py (MAPLIST) + docs/findings/2026-08-12-fship12-md8-5-mid53-nrcrlb.md", "output": "<thinking>\nThe finding note names three field ids explicitly: 67 (fship_12, the field\n        whose MAPJUMP fires the transition), 731 (md8_5, the destination field whose\n        dir/0 script contains the broken PMVIE), and 71 (fship_24, a sibling field\n        mentioned as part of the same ship-interior map family). These three are\n        verified directly against `MAPLIST`'s source positions, so a partial dict\n        keyed on just these ids is both correct and sufficient for reasoning about\n        this specific finding without needing the entire array in-line.\n</thinking>\n```python\nMAPLIST_PARTIAL = {67: \"fship_12\", 731: \"md8_5\", 71: \"fship_24\"}\nSTEM_TO_ID_PARTIAL = {v.upper(): k for k, v in MAPLIST_PARTIAL.items()}\n\nassert MAPLIST_PARTIAL[67].upper() == \"FSHIP_12\"\nassert MAPLIST_PARTIAL[731].upper() == \"MD8_5\"\nassert STEM_TO_ID_PARTIAL[\"FSHIP_24\"] == 71\n```"}
{"instruction": "Field id 0 in MAPLIST is the string 'dummy'. Does stem_for_field_id(0) return None or 'DUMMY', and why does that matter for id-validity checks?", "input": "scripts/field_maplist.py (stem_for_field_id, MAPLIST[0] = 'dummy')", "output": "<thinking>\n`stem_for_field_id` returns `None` only when the *string* at that index is\n        falsy (empty) or the index is out of range — `'dummy'` is a non-empty string,\n        so `stem_for_field_id(0)` returns `'DUMMY'`, not `None`. This matters because\n        a naive \"is this id valid?\" check using `if stem_for_field_id(id) is None`\n        will treat id 0 as valid even though 'dummy' is a placeholder/unused slot,\n        not a real playable field — callers that care about \"is this a real field\"\n        need an explicit id != 0 check in addition to the None check, since the\n        function's None-ness only encodes \"empty array slot\", not \"not a real field\".\n</thinking>\n```python\nfrom __future__ import annotations\n\nMAPLIST_PARTIAL = {0: \"dummy\", 637: \"loslake1\"}\n\ndef stem_for_field_id_partial(field_id: int) -> str | None:\n    name = MAPLIST_PARTIAL.get(field_id)\n    return name.upper() if name else None\n\ndef is_real_field(field_id: int) -> bool:\n    stem = stem_for_field_id_partial(field_id)\n    return stem is not None and field_id != 0  # exclude the 'dummy' placeholder slot\n\nassert stem_for_field_id_partial(0) == \"DUMMY\"   # not None!\nassert is_real_field(0) is False\nassert is_real_field(637) is True\n```"}
{"instruction": "BLACKBG4 is referenced as the debug hub field affected by the CSR single-disc CANONON/NRCRLB movie-id side effect. What field id does it correspond to, and how do you look it up in reverse from the stem?", "input": "scripts/field_maplist.py (MAPLIST/STEM_TO_ID) + docs/findings/2026-08-12-fship12-md8-5-mid53-nrcrlb.md", "output": "<thinking>\nCounting positions in the verified `MAPLIST` source, 'blackbg4' sits at index\n        96. `STEM_TO_ID` in `field_maplist.py` is built as\n        `{name.upper(): i for i, name in enumerate(MAPLIST) if name}`, i.e. an\n        uppercased-stem-to-index reverse map skipping empty slots — reproducing just\n        that one entry plus the dummy-guarding comprehension pattern gives a\n        self-contained reverse lookup without the full array.\n</thinking>\n```python\nMAPLIST_PARTIAL = {96: \"blackbg4\"}\nSTEM_TO_ID_PARTIAL = {name.upper(): i for i, name in MAPLIST_PARTIAL.items() if name}\n\nassert STEM_TO_ID_PARTIAL[\"BLACKBG4\"] == 96\n```"}
{"instruction": "EALS_1 (the field behind the EALS_1.DAT directory-record-growth fix) — what's its field id, and how would you cross-reference that id against a MAPJUMP operand found during a script scan?", "input": "scripts/field_maplist.py (MAPLIST) + notes/2026-07-28-csr-plus-increment-pivot.md", "output": "<thinking>\n'eals_1' sits at index 187 in the verified `MAPLIST` source. Cross-referencing\n        a MAPJUMP's field-id operand against a known stem is a simple equality check\n        once both are resolved through the same partial table — this is the same\n        confirm-the-target pattern used for the FSHIP_12->MD8_5 transition (matching\n        the MAPJUMP operand against `STEM_TO_ID['MD8_5'] == 731`), just applied to a\n        different field relevant to the ISO-growth note instead of the movie-id note.\n</thinking>\n```python\nMAPLIST_PARTIAL = {187: \"eals_1\"}\nSTEM_TO_ID_PARTIAL = {v.upper(): k for k, v in MAPLIST_PARTIAL.items()}\n\ndef mapjump_targets_eals1(mapjump_operands: list[int]) -> bool:\n    # True if any scanned MAPJUMP operand targets EALS_1.\n    return STEM_TO_ID_PARTIAL[\"EALS_1\"] in mapjump_operands\n\nassert mapjump_targets_eals1([187, 5, 9]) is True\nassert mapjump_targets_eals1([5, 9]) is False\n```"}
{"instruction": "NIVL (Nibelheim) — confirm its field id and show a self-contained round-trip (id -> stem -> id) without importing field_maplist.py.", "input": "scripts/field_maplist.py (MAPLIST/STEM_TO_ID)", "output": "<thinking>\n'nivl' sits at index 282 in the verified `MAPLIST` source. A round-trip\n        (id -> stem -> id) is a good sanity check for any partial-table snippet:\n        it confirms both the forward array and the reverse dict were built from the\n        exact same source entry, catching transcription errors before they leak into\n        downstream logic (e.g. a MAPJUMP validator that trusts a mistyped id).\n</thinking>\n```python\nMAPLIST_PARTIAL = {282: \"nivl\"}\nSTEM_TO_ID_PARTIAL = {v.upper(): k for k, v in MAPLIST_PARTIAL.items()}\n\nfield_id = 282\nstem = MAPLIST_PARTIAL[field_id].upper()\nround_trip_id = STEM_TO_ID_PARTIAL[stem]\n\nassert stem == \"NIVL\"\nassert round_trip_id == field_id\n```"}
{"instruction": "How many entries does the real MAPLIST array have, and why can't a fine-tuned model reliably guess an arbitrary field id's stem without the actual table?", "input": "scripts/field_maplist.py (MAPLIST, ~790 entries, from Makou Data::_mapList)", "output": "<thinking>\nThe verified `MAPLIST` array runs from index 0 ('dummy') through the low-700s\n        with map-family blocks (wm*, fship_*, blackbg*, md8_*, etc.) in a\n        non-alphabetic, non-formulaic order dictated purely by the original PSX\n        engine's disc-build order — there's no arithmetic relationship between a\n        field id and its stem (unlike, say, a sorted or hashed table). This means\n        any code claiming to resolve an *arbitrary* id must either ship the real\n        array or explicitly fail closed; guessing is guaranteed wrong outside the\n        handful of ids a snippet happens to hardcode.\n</thinking>\n```python\nMAPLIST_PARTIAL = {0: \"dummy\", 67: \"fship_12\", 637: \"loslake1\"}  # tiny known-good sample\n\ndef stem_for_field_id_safe(field_id: int) -> str:\n    if field_id not in MAPLIST_PARTIAL:\n        raise NotImplementedError(\n            f\"field id {field_id} not in this partial sample; the real MAPLIST \"\n            \"in scripts/field_maplist.py has ~790 non-formulaic entries — \"\n            \"do not guess a stem for an id outside a known table\"\n        )\n    return MAPLIST_PARTIAL[field_id].upper()\n\nassert stem_for_field_id_safe(67) == \"FSHIP_12\"\n```"}
{"instruction": "Show a fully self-contained function to read a little-endian uint32 from an ISO9660 directory record, matching psx_mode2_iso.py's _u32_le, and use it to pull a record's LBA (offset +2) and byte-size (offset +10).", "input": "scripts/psx_mode2_iso.py::_u32_le", "output": "<thinking>\nISO9660 directory records store the extent LBA at byte offset +2 (both LE and\n        BE copies per the spec, but the engine/tooling here reads the LE copy) and the\n        byte-size at offset +10 (also LE+BE dual copies, patched together in\n        `patch_dir_record_size`). `_u32_le` in `psx_mode2_iso.py` is a tiny four-byte\n        manual unpack (equivalent to `struct.unpack_from('<I', b, i)[0]` but written\n        without importing struct) — small enough to copy verbatim rather than import.\n</thinking>\n```python\ndef u32_le(b: bytes, i: int) -> int:\n    # Verbatim from psx_mode2_iso.py::_u32_le.\n    return b[i] | (b[i + 1] << 8) | (b[i + 2] << 16) | (b[i + 3] << 24)\n\ndef record_lba_and_size(rec: bytes) -> tuple[int, int]:\n    return u32_le(rec, 2), u32_le(rec, 10)\n\n# Example: a minimal synthetic 34-byte directory record with LBA=100, size=2048\nrec = bytearray(34)\nrec[2:6] = (100).to_bytes(4, \"little\")\nrec[10:14] = (2048).to_bytes(4, \"little\")\nassert record_lba_and_size(bytes(rec)) == (100, 2048)\n```"}
{"instruction": "Reproduce psx_mode2_iso.py's patch_dir_record_size logic in a fully self-contained function: given a raw directory-record byte blob and a new size, patch both the little-endian (+10) and big-endian (+14) size fields in place.", "input": "scripts/psx_mode2_iso.py::patch_dir_record_size", "output": "<thinking>\nISO9660 directory records store the byte-size redundantly in both endiannesses\n        for cross-platform readers: little-endian 4 bytes at offset +10, big-endian 4\n        bytes at offset +14. The real `patch_dir_record_size` operates on a whole\n        2048-byte sector buffer using `meta.dir_lba`/`meta.rec_offset` to locate the\n        record, but the core byte-patching logic is only two slice assignments and is\n        small enough to reproduce exactly, operating directly on the record bytes.\n</thinking>\n```python\ndef patch_record_size(rec: bytearray, new_size: int) -> None:\n    # Verbatim core logic from psx_mode2_iso.py::patch_dir_record_size,\n    # operating directly on a directory-record byte buffer instead of a full sector.\n    rec[10:14] = new_size.to_bytes(4, \"little\")\n    rec[14:18] = new_size.to_bytes(4, \"big\")\n\nrec = bytearray(34)\nrec[10:14] = (10192).to_bytes(4, \"little\")\nrec[14:18] = (10192).to_bytes(4, \"big\")\npatch_record_size(rec, 10200)\nassert int.from_bytes(rec[10:14], \"little\") == 10200\nassert int.from_bytes(rec[14:18], \"big\") == 10200\n```"}
{"instruction": "Write a self-contained sector-count helper equivalent to the ceil-division check in psx_mode2_iso.py::replace_file that decides whether a new file still fits its originally-allocated sector span.", "input": "scripts/psx_mode2_iso.py::replace_file (ceil(size/2048) capacity check)", "output": "<thinking>\n`replace_file` computes `old_slots = -(-meta.size // USER)` and\n        `new_slots = -(-len(new_data) // USER)` with `USER = 2048` — the\n        double-negation is Python's classic ceiling-division-via-floor-division idiom\n        (`-(-a // b) == ceil(a / b)` for positive integers). Growth is allowed only\n        when `new_slots <= old_slots`; otherwise it must raise, since writing past the\n        allocated extent would corrupt whatever file/directory follows on disc.\n</thinking>\n```python\nUSER = 2048  # ISO9660 Form1 logical sector user-data size\n\ndef sectors_needed(byte_size: int) -> int:\n    return -(-byte_size // USER)  # ceil(byte_size / USER)\n\ndef fits_in_place(old_size: int, new_size: int) -> bool:\n    return sectors_needed(new_size) <= sectors_needed(old_size)\n\nassert sectors_needed(10192) == 5\nassert sectors_needed(10200) == 5\nassert fits_in_place(10192, 10200) is True   # EALS_1.DAT case: fits, in-place patch OK\nassert fits_in_place(10192, 10241) is False  # crosses into a 6th sector: needs rebuild\n```"}
{"instruction": "Reproduce the ISO9660 directory-record parsing loop (as in psx_mode2_iso.py::_parse_dir_records) as a self-contained generator, including the zero-length-record sector-boundary skip and the '.'/'..' pseudo-entry skip.", "input": "scripts/psx_mode2_iso.py::_parse_dir_records", "output": "<thinking>\nDirectory record parsing must handle three quirks verbatim: (1) a record with\n        length byte 0 signals padding to the next 2048-byte sector boundary within the\n        extent, not end-of-data; (2) records are variable-length (length byte at\n        offset 0), so the cursor advances by that record's own length, not a fixed\n        stride; (3) the special '.' and '..' entries have `name_len == 1` and a name\n        byte of 0x00 or 0x01 and must be skipped, or they'd be mistaken for real files.\n</thinking>\n```python\nUSER = 2048\n\ndef parse_dir_records(blob: bytes):\n    # Verbatim logic from psx_mode2_iso.py::_parse_dir_records.\n    # Yields (name, lba, size, is_dir) for each real entry (skips '.'/'..').\n    def u32_le(b, i):\n        return b[i] | (b[i+1] << 8) | (b[i+2] << 16) | (b[i+3] << 24)\n\n    i = 0\n    while i < len(blob):\n        length = blob[i]\n        if length == 0:\n            nxt = ((i // USER) + 1) * USER  # skip padding to next sector\n            if nxt <= i:\n                break\n            i = nxt\n            continue\n        if i + length > len(blob):\n            break\n        rec = blob[i:i + length]\n        flags = rec[25]\n        name_len = rec[32]\n        if name_len == 1 and rec[33] in (0x00, 0x01):\n            i += length\n            continue  # '.' or '..'\n        name = rec[33:33 + name_len].split(b\";\", 1)[0].decode(\"ascii\", \"replace\").upper()\n        yield name, u32_le(rec, 2), u32_le(rec, 10), bool(flags & 0x02)\n        i += length\n```"}
{"instruction": "What are the raw byte offsets that make up a 2352-byte PSX MODE2 sector (sync/header/subheader/user/EDC region), reproduced without importing psx_mode2_iso.py?", "input": "scripts/psx_mode2_iso.py (SECTOR/USER/USER_OFF constants) + scripts/repair_mode2_edc.py", "output": "<thinking>\n`psx_mode2_iso.py` defines `SECTOR = 2352`, `USER = 2048`, and\n        `USER_OFF = 24` (sync 12 bytes + header 4 bytes + subheader 8 bytes) for the\n        Form1 view it cares about. The EDC field for Form1 (per the earlier verified\n        ImgBurn-verify-fix row) sits right after user data, at `USER_OFF + USER = 2072`.\n        Bundling these as one small offsets dict makes the sector layout self-contained\n        for any snippet that needs to seek within a raw `.bin` sector without importing\n        the module.\n</thinking>\n```python\nMODE2_FORM1_OFFSETS = {\n    \"sync\": (0, 12),\n    \"header\": (12, 16),\n    \"subheader\": (16, 24),\n    \"user\": (24, 24 + 2048),   # 24..2072\n    \"edc\": (2072, 2076),\n}\nSECTOR = 2352\n\ndef slice_sector(raw_sector: bytes, field: str) -> bytes:\n    assert len(raw_sector) == SECTOR\n    start, end = MODE2_FORM1_OFFSETS[field]\n    return raw_sector[start:end]\n\nassert MODE2_FORM1_OFFSETS[\"edc\"][0] == 2072  # matches the ImgBurn-verify LBA 614 offset\n```"}
{"instruction": "Reproduce IsoFile as a self-contained dataclass (matching psx_mode2_iso.py) and show why dir_lba/rec_offset default to -1 rather than None.", "input": "scripts/psx_mode2_iso.py::IsoFile", "output": "<thinking>\n`IsoFile` is a frozen dataclass with `path`, `lba`, `size`, and two\n        directory-record-location fields (`dir_lba`, `rec_offset`) that default to\n        `-1` rather than `None`. `-1` is used because these fields are consumed by\n        arithmetic later (`meta.dir_lba + (meta.rec_offset // USER)` inside\n        `patch_dir_record_size`), so a caller that forgot to check \"was this from\n        `find_file` (which fills them in) or `list_dir`'s older codepaths\" would get a\n        loud, obviously-wrong negative LBA instead of a `TypeError` from `None // int`\n        buried deeper in the call stack — a defensive default chosen for a specific\n        failure mode, not just an arbitrary sentinel.\n</thinking>\n```python\nfrom dataclasses import dataclass\n\n@dataclass(frozen=True)\nclass IsoFile:\n    # Verbatim from psx_mode2_iso.py::IsoFile.\n    path: str\n    lba: int\n    size: int\n    dir_lba: int = -1\n    rec_offset: int = -1\n\ndef require_patchable(meta: IsoFile) -> None:\n    if meta.dir_lba < 0 or meta.rec_offset < 0:\n        raise ValueError(f\"{meta.path}: no directory-record location (use find_file, not list_dir)\")\n\nok = IsoFile(path=\"FIELD/EALS_1.DAT\", lba=55003, size=10192, dir_lba=55000, rec_offset=68)\nrequire_patchable(ok)  # does not raise\n\nbad = IsoFile(path=\"FIELD/EALS_1.DAT\", lba=55003, size=10192)  # defaults -1, -1\ntry:\n    require_patchable(bad)\n    assert False\nexcept ValueError:\n    pass\n```"}
{"instruction": "Show a fully self-contained struct definition for a single MOVIE_ID.BIN row, and pack a new row for a movie at LBA 250450 with a Form2 engine size for 100 sectors.", "input": "docs/reference/movie-system.md (MOVIE_ID.BIN 20-byte LE row: LBA + engine_size + auxA/B/C)", "output": "<thinking>\nEach `MOVIE_ID.BIN` row is a fixed 20-byte little-endian record: LBA (4\n        bytes), engine_size (4 bytes, Form2 length = sectors*2336 — NOT the ISO9660\n        byte size), and three 4-byte aux fields whose exact semantics are unconfirmed\n        but which must be copied verbatim from a known-good source row rather than\n        zeroed, per `inject_movies_by_disc_id.py`'s docstring. `struct.pack(\"<IIIII\", ...)`\n        reproduces this exactly without needing any local module.\n</thinking>\n```python\nimport struct\n\ndef pack_movie_id_row(lba: int, engine_size: int, aux_a: int = 0, aux_b: int = 0, aux_c: int = 0) -> bytes:\n    return struct.pack(\"<IIIII\", lba, engine_size, aux_a, aux_b, aux_c)\n\nrow_bytes = pack_movie_id_row(lba=250450, engine_size=100 * 2336)\nassert len(row_bytes) == 20\nassert struct.unpack_from(\"<I\", row_bytes, 0)[0] == 250450\n```"}
{"instruction": "Given a raw MOVIE_ID.BIN blob, self-containedly iterate all rows and find any two disc-local movie ids that point at the same LBA (a collision, as happened with FSHIP_12/MD8_5 id 53).", "input": "docs/reference/movie-system.md + docs/findings/2026-08-12-fship12-md8-5-mid53-nrcrlb.md", "output": "<thinking>\nTwo different PMVIE ids pointing at the same LBA isn't inherently a bug (id 0\n        vs a genuinely-shared asset could be legitimate), but it *is* the exact\n        mechanism behind the FSHIP_12->MD8_5 finding: after single-disc-ing D2 content\n        onto D1, slot 53 still held D1's original LBA rather than D2's `NRCRLB.MOV`\n        LBA. Detecting *all* LBA collisions across a table is a cheap way to audit a\n        patched `MOVIE_ID.BIN` for accidental aliasing after a movie-injection pass.\n</thinking>\n```python\nimport struct\n\ndef iter_movie_id_rows(blob: bytes):\n    for i in range(0, len(blob) - len(blob) % 20, 20):\n        lba, engine_size, a, b, c = struct.unpack_from(\"<IIIII\", blob, i)\n        yield i // 20, lba, engine_size\n\ndef find_lba_collisions(blob: bytes) -> dict[int, list[int]]:\n    by_lba: dict[int, list[int]] = {}\n    for movie_id, lba, _size in iter_movie_id_rows(blob):\n        by_lba.setdefault(lba, []).append(movie_id)\n    return {lba: ids for lba, ids in by_lba.items() if len(ids) > 1}\n\n# Synthetic example: ids 12 and 53 both point at LBA 999999 (a collision)\nimport struct as _s\nblob = b\"\".join(\n    _s.pack(\"<IIIII\", 999999 if i in (12, 53) else i * 1000, 100 * 2336, 0, 0, 0)\n    for i in range(60)\n)\ncollisions = find_lba_collisions(blob)\nassert collisions == {999999: [12, 53]}\n```"}
{"instruction": "Write a self-contained function to patch a single MOVIE_ID.BIN row in place inside a bytearray image, given the row index and new (lba, engine_size) values, preserving the existing aux fields.", "input": "mods/single-disc/scripts/inject_movies_by_disc_id.py (movie table patch pattern)", "output": "<thinking>\nPatching one row must preserve the three aux fields (unconfirmed semantics,\n        so never zero them — copy or leave as-is) while only overwriting LBA and\n        engine_size. Reading the existing aux bytes first and re-packing the full\n        20-byte row keeps this safe even without knowing what the aux fields do.\n</thinking>\n```python\nimport struct\n\ndef patch_movie_id_row(movie_id_bin: bytearray, movie_id: int, new_lba: int, new_engine_size: int) -> None:\n    off = movie_id * 20\n    _old_lba, _old_size, aux_a, aux_b, aux_c = struct.unpack_from(\"<IIIII\", movie_id_bin, off)\n    movie_id_bin[off:off + 20] = struct.pack(\"<IIIII\", new_lba, new_engine_size, aux_a, aux_b, aux_c)\n\nbuf = bytearray(struct.pack(\"<IIIII\", 111, 222, 7, 8, 9) + b\"\\x00\" * 20)  # 2 rows\npatch_movie_id_row(buf, movie_id=0, new_lba=250450, new_engine_size=100 * 2336)\nlba, size, a, b, c = struct.unpack_from(\"<IIIII\", buf, 0)\nassert (lba, size, a, b, c) == (250450, 100 * 2336, 7, 8, 9)  # aux preserved\n```"}
{"instruction": "Self-contained: compute the Form2 engine_size fallback (sectors*2336) for a movie of a given ISO9660 byte size, when no source-disc row is available to copy from.", "input": "mods/single-disc/scripts/inject_movies_by_disc_id.py (_form2_size_field fallback)", "output": "<thinking>\nPer `inject_movies_by_disc_id.py`'s documented rule, the *preferred* path is\n        always copying a known-good source row's engine_size verbatim; the\n        `sectors*2336` formula is explicitly a fallback for when no source row\n        exists. Getting this wrong (e.g. using the ISO9660 byte size directly)\n        under-reports stream length and can cause the player to stop early or\n        misbehave mid-playback.\n</thinking>\n```python\ndef form2_engine_size_fallback(iso_byte_size: int) -> int:\n    USER = 2048  # ISO9660 logical sector size used for the directory record's byte-size field\n    nsec = -(-iso_byte_size // USER)  # ceil division\n    return nsec * 2336  # Form2 payload bytes per sector\n\nassert form2_engine_size_fallback(2048 * 100) == 100 * 2336\n# Prefer, when available: eng_size = struct.unpack_from(\"<I\", src_movie_id_bin, src_row*20+4)[0]\n```"}
{"instruction": "Self-contained: given old and new ISO9660 sizes for a shrunk MOVIE slot, compute how many trailing Form2 sectors are now residual (stale) and need zeroing.", "input": "mods/single-disc/scripts/inject_movies_by_disc_id.py::_zero_residual_sectors", "output": "<thinking>\nA shrink leaves `old_sectors - new_sectors` Form2 sectors physically present\n        past the new stream's logical end. `_zero_residual_sectors` is opt-in\n        (`FF7_ZERO_MOVIE_RESIDUAL=1`) defense-in-depth against decoders that read\n        past the declared engine_size; the sector-count math itself is a plain\n        ceil-division difference and needs no import to reproduce.\n</thinking>\n```python\nUSER = 2048\n\ndef sectors_for(byte_size: int) -> int:\n    return -(-byte_size // USER)\n\ndef residual_sector_count(old_iso_size: int, new_iso_size: int) -> int:\n    old_n, new_n = sectors_for(old_iso_size), sectors_for(new_iso_size)\n    return max(0, old_n - new_n)\n\nassert residual_sector_count(old_iso_size=2048 * 100, new_iso_size=2048 * 60) == 40\nassert residual_sector_count(old_iso_size=2048 * 60, new_iso_size=2048 * 100) == 0  # growth, nothing residual\n```"}
{"instruction": "Self-contained: resolve a PMVIE opcode's movie id all the way to a filename, given a synthetic MOVIE_ID.BIN blob and a {lba: filename} dict built from the ISO's MOVIE/ directory — and show why sorted(dirents)[id] is wrong.", "input": "docs/reference/movie-system.md (PMVIE -> MOVIE_ID.BIN row -> LBA -> ISO dirent chain)", "output": "<thinking>\nThe only correct resolution path is PMVIE id -> `MOVIE_ID.BIN` row -> LBA ->\n        join against the ISO's actual dirent-by-LBA mapping. `sorted(MOVIE/\n        dirents)[pmvie_id]` was a previously-shipped bug verified wrong for every one\n        of 61 D2 rows checked (alphabetical dirent order has no relationship to\n        disc-local movie id order), so a self-contained resolver must go through the\n        LBA join, never a sorted-index shortcut.\n</thinking>\n```python\nimport struct\n\ndef resolve_pmvie(movie_id_bin: bytes, dirents_by_lba: dict[int, str], pmvie_id: int) -> str:\n    lba, _size, _a, _b, _c = struct.unpack_from(\"<IIIII\", movie_id_bin, pmvie_id * 20)\n    return dirents_by_lba[lba]  # KeyError => table/dirent out of sync, do not guess\n\nblob = struct.pack(\"<IIIII\", 129252, 100 * 2336, 0, 0, 0)  # row 0\ndirents_by_lba = {129252: \"FSHIP2.BIN\"}  # NOT alphabetically first, confirmed by finding\n\nassert resolve_pmvie(blob, dirents_by_lba, pmvie_id=0) == \"FSHIP2.BIN\"\n# WRONG (previously-shipped bug): sorted(dirents_by_lba.values())[0] would pick\n# whatever sorts first alphabetically, unrelated to the LBA row 0 actually points at.\n```"}
{"instruction": "Self-contained: write the MODE2 Form1 sector-type detection check (sync/mode/frame bytes) used by repair_mode2_edc.py, without importing it.", "input": "scripts/repair_mode2_edc.py::_is_mode2_form1", "output": "<thinking>\nPer the Yellow Book CD-ROM spec, a MODE2 sector's 12-byte sync pattern is\n        `00 FF FF FF FF FF FF FF FF FF FF 00`, and the header's mode byte (sector[15])\n        is 2 for MODE2. `repair_mode2_edc.py` checks sync byte 0 == 0, sector[11] == 0,\n        sector[15] == 2, and bytes 1..10 all 0xFF — matching only Form1 sectors so\n        Form2/XA streaming sectors (which don't carry EDC/ECC the same way) are left\n        untouched during checksum repair.\n</thinking>\n```python\ndef is_mode2_form1(sec: bytes) -> bool:\n    if sec[0] != 0 or sec[11] != 0 or sec[15] != 2:\n        return False\n    return all(sec[i] == 0xFF for i in range(1, 11))\n\ngood = bytes([0x00] + [0xFF] * 10 + [0x00] * 4 + [0x02]) + bytes(2352 - 16)\nassert is_mode2_form1(good) is True\n\nbad_mode = bytes([0x00] + [0xFF] * 10 + [0x00] * 4 + [0x01]) + bytes(2352 - 16)  # mode byte wrong\nassert is_mode2_form1(bad_mode) is False\n```"}
{"instruction": "Self-contained: given a raw pristine .bin image and a modified copy, find every LBA whose 2048-byte Form1 user data actually changed (the set repair_mode2_edc.py must regenerate EDC/ECC for).", "input": "scripts/repair_mode2_edc.py (selective EDC/ECC regen: only sectors whose user data differs)", "output": "<thinking>\n`repair_mode2_edc.py`'s whole selective-repair strategy hinges on comparing\n        only the 2048-byte user-data window (offset 24..2072 within each 2352-byte\n        sector) between pristine and modified images — not the full sector, since the\n        EDC/ECC footer is expected to differ (that's the whole point of the tool).\n        Diffing user-data windows gives exactly the LBA set that needs real\n        checksums recomputed; everything else can have its footer restored verbatim\n        from pristine.\n</thinking>\n```python\nSECTOR, USER_OFF, USER = 2352, 24, 2048\n\ndef changed_lbas(pristine: bytes, modified: bytes) -> list[int]:\n    assert len(pristine) == len(modified) and len(pristine) % SECTOR == 0\n    out = []\n    for lba in range(len(pristine) // SECTOR):\n        off = lba * SECTOR + USER_OFF\n        if pristine[off:off + USER] != modified[off:off + USER]:\n            out.append(lba)\n    return out\n\np = bytearray(SECTOR * 3)\nm = bytearray(p)\nm[1 * SECTOR + USER_OFF] = 0xAB  # change 1 byte of sector 1's user data\nassert changed_lbas(bytes(p), bytes(m)) == [1]\n```"}
{"instruction": "Self-contained: verify the ImgBurn-verify-failure symptom (zeroed EDC footer, byte-identical user data) can be detected purely from a single sector's bytes, without needing the pristine image at all.", "input": "notes/2026-07-27-imgburn-verify-yamada.md (offset 2072 EDC field, all-zero == suspicious)", "output": "<thinking>\nA genuinely all-zero EDC/ECC footer is a strong (though not 100% certain —\n        a real CRC32-like EDC over specific data could theoretically be zero) signal\n        of a builder bug rather than valid data, since a real checksum landing on\n        exactly zero is astronomically unlikely for real user data. This is useful as\n        a fast single-image triage pass before doing the more expensive pristine-diff\n        described in the previous row.\n</thinking>\n```python\nSECTOR, EDC_OFF, EDC_LEN = 2352, 2072, 4\n\ndef has_suspicious_zero_edc(sector: bytes) -> bool:\n    assert len(sector) == SECTOR\n    return sector[EDC_OFF:EDC_OFF + EDC_LEN] == b\"\\x00\" * EDC_LEN\n\nsector = bytearray(SECTOR)  # all zero, including EDC field\nassert has_suspicious_zero_edc(bytes(sector)) is True\nsector[EDC_OFF:EDC_OFF + 4] = b\"\\xcc\\xa1\\xb4\\x64\"  # pristine's real EDC value from the note\nassert has_suspicious_zero_edc(bytes(sector)) is False\n```"}
{"instruction": "Self-contained: given a target byte offset within a raw 2352-byte-per-sector .bin image (e.g. offset 2072 at LBA 614, from the ImgBurn note), compute which LBA and in-sector offset that corresponds to.", "input": "notes/2026-07-27-imgburn-verify-yamada.md (LBA 614, offset 2072 = EDC field)", "output": "<thinking>\nConverting an absolute file offset in a raw MODE2/2352 image to (LBA,\n        in-sector offset) is a plain divmod by the sector size — the note's\n        \"LBA 614, offset 2072\" description is exactly `divmod(614 * 2352 + 2072, 2352)`.\n        This is the inverse of the seek math used everywhere else in this repo's ISO\n        tooling (`lba * SECTOR + USER_OFF` to seek *to* a known LBA).\n</thinking>\n```python\nSECTOR = 2352\n\ndef offset_to_lba(abs_offset: int) -> tuple[int, int]:\n    return divmod(abs_offset, SECTOR)  # (lba, offset_within_sector)\n\ndef lba_to_offset(lba: int, in_sector_offset: int = 0) -> int:\n    return lba * SECTOR + in_sector_offset\n\nabs_off = lba_to_offset(614, 2072)\nassert offset_to_lba(abs_off) == (614, 2072)\n```"}
{"instruction": "End to end, self-contained: parse a synthetic field script byte blob containing a MAPJUMP to field id 731 (MD8_5) followed by RET, using only inline partial tables (no imports of ff7_opcodes.py or field_maplist.py).", "input": "scripts/ff7_opcodes.py + scripts/field_maplist.py (combined, corrected MAPJUMP=0x60)", "output": "<thinking>\nCombining the corrected MAPJUMP opcode value (0x60, length 6 — see the\n        earlier correction row) with a partial MAPLIST entry for id 731 gives a fully\n        self-contained decode of \"this script jumps to MD8_5\" without any local\n        imports, using only literal bytes for the synthetic script.\n</thinking>\n```python\nfrom __future__ import annotations\n\nOPCODE_LENGTH_PARTIAL = {0x00: 1, 0x60: 6}  # RET, MAPJUMP (corrected value)\nMAPLIST_PARTIAL = {731: \"md8_5\"}\n\ndef decode_mapjump_script(data: bytes) -> str | None:\n    off = 0\n    while off < len(data):\n        op = data[off]\n        if op == 0x60:\n            # field_id > 255 (e.g. 731) needs 2 bytes; MAPJUMP's 6-byte total length\n            # is opcode(1) + field_id(2 LE) + 3 more positional/orientation bytes.\n            field_id = data[off + 1] | (data[off + 2] << 8)\n            return MAPLIST_PARTIAL.get(field_id, f\"<unknown field id {field_id}>\").upper()\n        if op == 0x00:\n            return None\n        off += OPCODE_LENGTH_PARTIAL.get(op, 1)\n    return None\n\nscript = bytes([0x60, 731 & 0xFF, (731 >> 8) & 0xFF, 0, 0, 0, 0x00])\nassert decode_mapjump_script(script) == \"MD8_5\"\n```"}
{"instruction": "Self-contained: verify a directory record's dual-endian size fields (LE @+10, BE @+14) agree, as a corruption sanity check before trusting either value.", "input": "scripts/psx_mode2_iso.py (dual-endian ISO9660 size field convention)", "output": "<thinking>\nISO9660 stores directory-record sizes redundantly in both little- and\n        big-endian for cross-platform readers. A mismatch between the two 4-byte\n        copies within the same record is a strong corruption signal (a naive patch\n        that updates only one endianness — unlike the real `patch_dir_record_size`,\n        which always updates both — would produce exactly this kind of mismatch),\n        so checking agreement is a cheap self-contained integrity check.\n</thinking>\n```python\ndef sizes_agree(rec: bytes) -> bool:\n    le = int.from_bytes(rec[10:14], \"little\")\n    be = int.from_bytes(rec[14:18], \"big\")\n    return le == be\n\ngood = bytearray(18)\ngood[10:14] = (10200).to_bytes(4, \"little\")\ngood[14:18] = (10200).to_bytes(4, \"big\")\nassert sizes_agree(bytes(good)) is True\n\ncorrupt = bytearray(good)\ncorrupt[10:14] = (9999).to_bytes(4, \"little\")  # only LE was patched — bug\nassert sizes_agree(bytes(corrupt)) is False\n```"}
{"instruction": "Self-contained: validate a batch of (lba, size) pairs read from FIELD.BIN's field-file index table for basic sanity (nonzero lba, nonzero size, monotonically non-decreasing lba) before trusting them for extraction.", "input": "Qhimm forum topic=6496.0 (FIELD.BIN index table, 2361 LE uint32 pairs at 0x0003A5B8)", "output": "<thinking>\nBefore seeking into an ISO based on an index table entry, cheap sanity checks\n        catch a misaligned read (e.g. off-by-one in the 8-byte stride, or reading\n        past the table's real 2361-entry bound) early: lba/size should never be zero\n        for a real field file, and since FIELD.BIN's table is generally laid out in\n        disc order, a lba that suddenly goes backwards after a large jump forward is\n        a red flag worth logging even though it isn't strictly guaranteed monotonic.\n</thinking>\n```python\nimport struct\n\ndef validate_field_index(blob: bytes, start: int = 0x0003A5B8, count: int = 2361) -> list[str]:\n    problems = []\n    prev_lba = -1\n    for i in range(count):\n        off = start + i * 8\n        lba, size = struct.unpack_from(\"<II\", blob, off)\n        if lba == 0 or size == 0:\n            problems.append(f\"entry {i}: zero lba/size ({lba}, {size})\")\n        if lba < prev_lba:\n            problems.append(f\"entry {i}: lba {lba} decreased from previous {prev_lba}\")\n        prev_lba = lba\n    return problems\n\nfake = struct.pack(\"<II\", 55000, 2048) + struct.pack(\"<II\", 0, 2048)  # 2nd entry bad\nissues = validate_field_index(fake, start=0, count=2)\nassert any(\"zero lba\" in p for p in issues)\n```"}
{"instruction": "What is the instruction length (in bytes, opcode byte included) of RET (0x00) in an FF7 field script?", "input": "scripts/ff7_opcodes.py (OPCODE_LENGTH, verified against Makou Reactor Opcode.cpp)", "output": "<thinking>\nRET is opcode 0x00. In the verified `OPCODE_LENGTH` list from\n        `scripts/ff7_opcodes.py` (index-aligned to the opcode byte, sourced\n        from Makou Reactor's `Opcode.cpp`), index 0x00 (0 decimal) holds\n        `1` -- 1 opcode byte plus 0 operand byte(s).\n</thinking>\n```python\n# Verified single-entry subset of OPCODE_LENGTH (ff7_opcodes.py / Opcode.cpp).\nOPCODE_LENGTH_PARTIAL = {0x00: 1}  # RET\n\ndef opcode_length(op: int) -> int:\n    return OPCODE_LENGTH_PARTIAL[op]\n\nassert opcode_length(0x00) == 1  # RET\n```"}
